WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.944
Picture the support desk at a café payments company.

2
00:00:05.944 --> 00:00:11.247
A customer's coffee was refunded yesterday, but support's screen still says captured.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.816
That screen reads a copy, rebuilt every night, so it's a day behind.

4
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:18.583
So the team stops waiting for the night.

5
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:23.300
Whenever a payment changes, the program updates the records, then the copy.

6
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.879
Two lines of code, and every test passes.

7
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:33.432
A month later, one payment says refunded in the records and captured on the screen, for good, with no error anywhere.

8
00:00:33.432 --> 00:00:36.301
How can two writes that both succeed disagree?

9
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:38.359
Picture the café itself.

10
00:00:38.359 --> 00:00:41.655
By the till, a book where every sale is written down.

11
00:00:41.655 --> 00:00:44.204
In the back, a board the staff read from.

12
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:50.108
One way to fill the board: after closing, the owner rewrites it from the day's receipts.

13
00:00:50.108 --> 00:00:51.839
Right, but only once a day.

14
00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:56.839
A program that works through a whole pile in one run like that is a batch job.

15
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.128
The other way: a helper copies each receipt onto the board as the till prints it.

16
00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:09.700
Each receipt is an event, a record of one thing that happened, and handling each event as it arrives is stream processing.

17
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.262
In the program, the book is the database, where each payment is stored.

18
00:01:14.262 --> 00:01:17.764
Here it's PostgreSQL, a free database many companies run.

19
00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:24.555
The board is a search index, a copy built for fast searching, like the index at the back of a book.

20
00:01:24.555 --> 00:01:28.244
It lives in OpenSearch, a free program for such indexes.

21
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:35.431
A payment's status goes from authorized, money set aside, to captured, money moved, and maybe refunded.

22
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.046
And there's no helper.

23
00:01:37.046 --> 00:01:42.860
Each request is a cashier who writes the book, then walks to the board and writes it there too.

24
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:53.389
Two requests for pay-981 arrive a millisecond apart, each on its own thread, a line of work running at the same time: a capture, and the shop's refund.

25
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.140
The capture writes the database: captured.

26
00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:01.704
Then it stalls for a couple of milliseconds, say while the program clears out unused memory.

27
00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:04.244
A cashier stopped on the way to the board.

28
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:07.350
The refund writes the database: refunded.

29
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.411
Right, the refund came second.

30
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:12.020
It writes the index too: refunded.

31
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.449
Then the capture wakes up and makes its second write.

32
00:02:15.449 --> 00:02:17.113
The index now says captured.

33
00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.355
Every call returned success.

34
00:02:19.355 --> 00:02:21.626
No error, no retry, no log line.

35
00:02:21.626 --> 00:02:26.880
And the index stays wrong until pay-981 changes again, which may be never.

36
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:31.955
Both cashiers did exactly what they were told, and the board is wrong anyway.

37
00:02:31.955 --> 00:02:34.442
Nobody to blame, which is somehow worse.

38
00:02:34.442 --> 00:02:37.427
The bug lives in the gap between the two writes.

39
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.073
Was that bad luck?

40
00:02:39.073 --> 00:02:44.872
Each request writes the database, then the index, so the four writes can mix in six ways.

41
00:02:44.872 --> 00:02:47.935
How many of the six leave the stores different?

42
00:02:47.935 --> 00:02:48.977
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:52.446
Two.

44
00:02:52.446 --> 00:02:58.580
The one we saw, and its mirror, where the database ends captured and the index refunded.

45
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:03.640
In the other four, whoever wrote the database last also wrote the index last.

46
00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.872
Nothing in the code makes that true.

47
00:03:05.872 --> 00:03:09.468
The timing usually does, and that's why every test passed.

48
00:03:09.733 --> 00:03:12.640
Four out of six is a fine score on a test.

49
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.440
For payments, not so much.

50
00:03:14.833 --> 00:03:17.024
You might think: we retry on errors.

51
00:03:17.024 --> 00:03:20.372
But nothing failed here, so there was nothing to retry.

52
00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:23.060
And a real failure opens a second door.

53
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:29.406
If the program crashes between the two lines, after a restart it doesn't know a second write was owed.

54
00:03:29.406 --> 00:03:32.579
One store has the change, and the other never will.

55
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:40.615
This pattern is called a dual write: one change sent to two systems, one after the other, with nothing tying them together.

56
00:03:40.867 --> 00:03:45.588
In Java and Kotlin, it's these two lines, and the gap sits between them.

57
00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:51.275
Both problems have one root: two writers, each deciding the order.

58
00:03:51.533 --> 00:03:52.603
Back to the café.

59
00:03:52.603 --> 00:03:54.805
Let only the till decide the order.

60
00:03:54.805 --> 00:04:02.668
It numbers each receipt, and the helper copies them in number order, noting the last number so it can carry on after a break.

61
00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:08.121
In the program, requests write only the database, and the database settles the race.

62
00:04:08.121 --> 00:04:11.580
The capture holds the row, so the refund waits its turn.

63
00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.729
Its log records the capture first, then the refund.

64
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:20.855
A follower applies that log to the index in order, keeping a bookmark of how far it got.

65
00:04:20.855 --> 00:04:22.687
Both stores end at refunded.

66
00:04:22.933 --> 00:04:26.967
The price is lag: the index hears each change a few milliseconds late.

67
00:04:26.967 --> 00:04:30.712
But it can't get the order wrong, because it no longer picks one.

68
00:04:30.967 --> 00:04:33.024
So, two writers pick two orders.

69
00:04:33.024 --> 00:04:36.881
Give the data one writer, and let every copy follow its log.

70
00:04:36.881 --> 00:04:40.224
Now the customer's refund shows on support's screen.

71
00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:41.680
One quick check.

72
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.254
With the follower in place, the program crashes right after the refund's database write.

73
00:04:47.254 --> 00:04:49.408
What does the index end up saying?

74
00:04:49.408 --> 00:04:50.421
Pause and think.

75
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.179
Refunded.

76
00:04:54.179 --> 00:04:59.389
The change is already in the log, and the follower picks it up from its bookmark.

77
00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:01.833
Nobody had to remember a second write.
